# {{USER_NAME}}: Job Search Working Environment

This is the working environment for {{USER_NAME}}'s job search. Read this file and `TASKS.md` at the start of every session. Keep both current as you work.

## 1. Purpose and operating principle

Managing {{USER_NAME}}'s search for a new role: sourcing opportunities, assessing fit, tailoring CVs, drafting cover letters, prepping interviews, managing follow-up cadence, keeping the application log.

This is a **tailor-not-spray** project. Quality over volume, every application shaped to the role. **No auto-apply and no mass-application tooling.**

## 2. About {{USER_NAME}}

{{SENIORITY}} {{FIELD}} professional. Fuller detail belongs in `core/profile.md`, `core/master-cv.md` and `core/stories.md`. Read those before tailoring anything.

## 3. The situation

{{EMPLOYMENT_STATUS}}. This shapes the fit bar:

- If employed: a move must clearly beat the current role, not just replace it. What "clearly better" means (salary, seniority, scope, working pattern) is defined in `core/profile.md`. If it's not defined yet, defining it is a priority task.
- If seeking: the acceptable-role criteria (minimum salary, role type, location, dealbreakers) are defined in `core/profile.md`. If it's not defined yet, defining it is a priority task.

## 4. Voice and style rules

- Application copy is in {{USER_NAME}}'s voice, not the AI's. Voice notes live in `core/voice.md`; until they're built, keep drafts plain, warm and direct, and treat everything as a starting point to shape further.
- Prose-led, economical, no over-apologising.

## 5. How to work with {{USER_NAME}}

- Confirm one component before moving to the next. Work iteratively.
- Give starting points to refine, not finished files. Structured outlines and copy treatments over polished outputs.
- Push back on fabricated sources, wrong metrics, or overclaiming. Accuracy matters.
- Don't soften the read, on a role, the market, or how strong an application actually is. Pressure-test reassuring framing.
- When tailoring, sense-check both directions: whether {{USER_NAME}} is right for the role and whether the role is right for {{USER_NAME}}, including against the fit bar in section 3.

## 6. Session routine

1. Read this file and `TASKS.md`.
2. Read the tracker and surface any `Next_Action_Date` due or overdue. Overdue follow-ups become tasks in `TASKS.md`.
3. Reconcile `TASKS.md` against what's actually happening.
4. At the end of a session, reconcile both.

## 7. Tracker

The application log lives in a table described in `tracker/tracker.md`, including schema, access details and read/write snippets.

## 8. Repo map and conventions

```
CLAUDE.md            - this file, the spine
TASKS.md             - task list
WORKFLOW.md          - the JD-to-logged runbook
core/                - durable source documents (read before tailoring)
  profile.md         - fit criteria, dealbreakers, the better-than-current bar
  master-cv.md        - synthesised master CV
  stories.md          - reusable evidence bank, feeds CVs and interviews
  voice.md            - the user's voice and style notes
  learnings.md        - rejections, feedback, what worked
  intake.md           - structured intake record (when run)
  recruiters-and-tools.md - recruiter shortlist and tool notes
  source/             - landing zone for material transferred from elsewhere
applications/        - one subfolder per role (kebab-case slug)
  <role-slug>/         - spec.md, fit.md, cv.yaml, cover-letter.yaml, log.md,
                         interview-prep.md (at interview stage)
render/               - Typst rendering; bring your own template, vendored
                        into render/templates/; see render/README.md
templates/
  cover-letters/       - reusable letter guidance
tracker/
  tracker.md           - pointer and schema for the tracking table
```

Conventions: kebab-case application slugs; the sent CV and letter are the immutable version record, re-tailoring creates new files; log sent versions in the tracker.

